⟦500⟧{{smaller block|''Meconic acid'', or oxypyrone tricarboxylic acid (3·2·6) C<sub>5</sub>HO<sub>2</sub>(OH)(CO<sub>2</sub>H)<sub>2</sub>, found in opium, crystallizes in prisms and gives a characteristic deep red colour with ferric chloride. On heating to 200° it gives comenic acid, C<sub>5</sub>H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>(OH)(CO<sub>2</sub>H), and on distillation pyromeconic acid or <math>\beta</math>-oxypyrone. On comenic acid see A. Peratoner, ''Gazz.'', 1906, 36 (i.), p. 1.}}

⟦700⟧{{smaller block|The tetrahydro-<math>\gamma</math>-pyrones may be obtained by the condensation of aldehydes with acetone-dicarboxylic ester in the presence of hydrochloric acid.}}

